1722	    essentially do this eigenvalue by eigenvalue.
1723	    To begin, we consider a hypothetical eigenvalue $d/k \leq p \leq 1$.
1724	    Observe that $F(p)$ is precisely the
1725	    probability 
1726	    probability of observing at least $d+1$ successes out of $k$
1727	    i.i.d.\ Bernoulli trials, each of which succeeds with probability
1728	    $p$.
1729	    In other words, it is the probability that $\bY := \bY_1 + \cdots + \bY_k \geq d+1$,
1730	    where $\bY_1, \ldots, \bY_k \sim \mathrm{Bernoulli}(p)$.
